sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-15> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Even systems governed by continuous equations, such as certain chemical reaction kinetics, commonly have a limited number of stable states with discrete transitions between them, and because discrete overall behavior is often a system's most obvious feature, discrete models like cellular automata are often the most appropriate.";
    dc:type "model-of-nature";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Continuous rules can yield discrete behavior, so discrete models often fit";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “The Problem of Satisfying Constraints” (from p. 342), p. 342. Scope: natural systems with continuous underlying dynamics but discrete overall behavior, such as phase transitions and bistable chemical reactions"
}
